## 7. REAL LUMÉA PRODUCT CATALOGUE INTEGRATION

This is one of the most important requirements.

Before implementing recommendations, locate and reuse the actual product data already present in the project.

Use the existing product data source, whether it is a local data file, API, database, or another established mechanism.

If no reusable source exists, create one centralised product catalogue using the project's existing conventions. Do not duplicate the same product information in multiple components.

Where available, each product record should include:

- Unique product ID.
- Product name.
- Actual price and currency.
- Product image path.
- Product detail URL or route.
- Product category.
- Product description.
- Documented skin types.
- Documented skin concerns.
- Ingredients.
- Intended use.
- Usage instructions.
- Known product warnings or relevant sensitivities.
- Availability or stock status.
- Variant information, where applicable.

Only use properties that actually exist or can be verified from the real product information. Never invent missing ingredients, benefits, prices, stock levels, or product claims.

If a field is missing, handle it gracefully. Do not show fabricated details to fill the space.

### Recommendation rules

1. Match products against the user's skin type, concern, goal, and stated preferences.
2. Use product metadata to filter or rank relevant products before presenting them.
3. Ask the AI to explain why a product may be relevant using verified catalogue information.
4. Validate all returned product IDs against the actual catalogue.
5. Render product names, prices, images, links, and availability from trusted product records, not from arbitrary AI-generated text.
6. Do not invent products, IDs, product URLs, prices, reviews, ratings, discounts, or stock.
7. Exclude unavailable products from purchase recommendations when stock information is available.
8. If no reliable match exists, tell the user that no suitable match was found in the current collection.
9. If the user provides insufficient information, ask a helpful follow-up question instead of pretending to know their needs.
10. Never guarantee results or claim that a product will cure a skin condition.

Show a small, sensible number of relevant products per recommendation, preferably two or three when enough genuine matches exist. Do not display every product in the catalogue in every response.

